&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Edited by: &lt;/strong&gt;Sean Sinico&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;US prosecutors have announced new charges against ousted Venezuelan President Nicolas Maduro and his wife, Cilia Flores, accusing them of conspiring to torture political opponents and US citizens.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The new charges against the couple are in addition to the drug-trafficking charges they previously faced, according to a revised indictment released Thursday.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Both Maduro and Flores have denied the previous charges brought against them.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Maduro's lawyer Barry Pollack said in a statement, &quot;President Maduro denies the charges in the new indictment and looks ​forward to his day &amp;zwnj;in court.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Revised indictment adds pressure on Maduro&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The revised indictment alleged that the couple committed human rights violations as part of a scheme to hold on to power.&lt;/p&gt;
